    "details": {
      "biography": {
        "value": "<p><strong>Renlow Veck</strong> — Daggerford shipping clerk who discovered what pays better than freight. He sold names to the hags: widows, mostly, people with nobody left to come asking. He is the man who murdered Thomas's wife Ann and their four children, <strong>six months ago</strong>. The Sharrans took him within weeks of that, which is why the Widows' Ledger entries stop about half a year back. He has been their prisoner ever since — six months in the dark, not more. <em>(Retconned 2026-09-06: the earlier \"year and a half\" was a table slip; Thomas's backstory says six months.)</em></p><p><strong>The portrait is him</strong> — the gilt-framed merchant painting out of the hag loot, painted fat and prosperous on murder money. Let a player make the connection; the item art is his face.</p><hr><p><strong>THE CONFESSION — read in the zone of truth.</strong> <em>He has had six months to arrange these words and nobody worth saying them to. Let it come out in pieces.</em></p><p>\"I sold names. That's how it started — only names. Widows, mostly. People with nobody left to come asking after them. It was money. It was only ever money.\"</p><p>\"Then a scribe in the ledger office started pulling threads. And he was careful, and he was slow, and he was not going to stop.\" <em>He will not look up.</em> \"So I went to his house to frighten him. That's the truth, and your spell will tell you it's the truth — I went there to put a fright into a careful man so he'd stop being careful.\"</p><p>\"It went wrong.\" <em>A long silence.</em> \"I'm not going to dress it up for you. It went wrong, and then it was too late to be anything but finished, and I finished it.\" <em>He gets as far as</em> the children <em>and stops, and cannot get past the word, and goes around it.</em> \"All of them. Yes. All of them.\"</p><p><em>And then — he does not hear himself say it:</em> \"It shouldn't have gone that way. He should have left it <em>alone</em>.\"</p><p>\"I'm sorry.\" <em>He says it to the floor.</em> \"I've said it every day for six months down here and it doesn't do anything, does it. None of it comes back. I'd give it back. If there were a way to give it back, I would give it back.\"</p><p><em>Every word of that is true. That is the horror of it.</em></p><hr><p><strong>Playing him:</strong> beaten, <strong>not</strong> redeemed. The sorrow is genuine — the zone proves it — and it is still mostly about <em>him</em>: the six months, the dark, the thing he has to carry. The line that gives him away is \"he should have left it alone,\" and he doesn't notice he said it. If someone calls him on it he'll agree instantly, sincerely, and mean that too. There is nothing here to punch and nothing to forgive — which is exactly what the Sharrans arranged. An execution, not a fight.</p><p>He begs. By name, if Thomas gives his. CR 0 commoner, no combat value, nameplate reads \"Prisoner\" (control-only).</p>",
        "public": ""
      },
